i'm jane secker  and you're listening to episode 4 of  storycast  21. in 2016 more than 5 000  migrants drowned in the mediterranean as  they tried to reach  europe among the 360 000 people who  attempted the crossing that year  in the biggest mass movement of people  since world war  ii was a family fleeing the war  in syria this is migrant rescue  my name's mark stone and i'm a reporter  a correspondent for sky news  it was january the 15th 2016.  i was in my house in brussels in belgium  just put our son to bed and i was  getting ready for what i thought would  be an uneventful evening  they started as they do every morning  here  looking for the dots on the horizon  some of the tiny migrant boats show up  on radar  some don't we're about five to eight  i got a uh a facebook message  and it was from a young man called basil  shakarni  he says hello mr stone and then there's  a long list of numbers  i told him now my family in this  location  do you have any information about the  weather there  he was in germany messaging me and i  immediately realized what's happening  this is his family who are on the coast  of  of turkey and they were trying to get to  a very small island called nera a greek  island and it's a distance  of around about eight miles so not very  far  but remember it's january the water's  cold and pretty rough  my name is zanzon khalid i'm 28 years  old  i come from syria  i was with basil's family his father  mother and twin sister they  show us the small light in the other  side of a greek island  and told us it's about one hour from  here  my wife and we talked to each other  many times at this day  i said to him the weather is too bad  i'm not sure that we can arrive safe and  my  goods feeling told me that something  will go wrong but we have no other  chance we are  required to do the journey now  i asked mark about the weather and he  told me  it's too risky they shouldn't go  [Music]  well these are the latest pictures  coming in now from russia in hungary  as you're just about to see police there  have been using  tear gas against vassal was a young guy  from the syrian capital damascus  he had been in the military at one point  uh he didn't want to be part of the  civil war in syria  and so he he left  i met him a few months earlier  in budapest in hungary  budapest at that time in late 2015 had  become  a bottleneck in in a migrant route that  was um  crossing europe the hundreds thousands  of migrants make their way  through a variety of countries and to  germany we saw that the spray  of tears bangla merkel the german  chancellor had made a  decision to open germany's borders and  to allow refugees to come in  another spray two sprays of the tear gas  into the faces this was a period of time  when hundreds and  hundreds and hundreds of people were  trying to make the journey  either across the aegean from turkey to  greece or across the  mediterranean from north africa to italy  massive challenge for the team now is  trying to make sure that everyone stays  seated  the previous year 2015 at least 3  700 people had died trying to cross  those waters  into europe so i knew  not just from the figures but from  having reported and covered  many other shipwrecks over the months  before  i knew how dangerous this crossing was  slowly the main rescue ship is  maneuvered towards the dinghy  he had told me that his twin sister his  fiancee at the time and his his parents  they would try and join him  in germany once he had managed to put  down some roots  it was about eight or seven o'clock  local time  we were about more than 40 persons  many families with many children  and just we shocked when we saw the boat  even it was difficult to see it  but they turned on light for short time  to check the  engine it might be  just for 10 people maximum  so the wind was strong and very cold  this wind created the huge waves  and the sounds of these waves you can  compare it to the sounds of the  airstrikes  that we heard in syria  i thought of my parents basel  my brothers my sister  and i think could i meet them again or  not  my wife started to send me the location  after one hour i used my laptop to make  a  line of locations  so i noticed that the direction  not to go to the greeks island  brothers they go in the wrong way  by the time this the next exchange of  messages came in it was  20 past 10 in brussels i by this stage  was in bed  he sent me a another set of coordinates  i copied and pasted the long number  into google maps and then this  pin dropped on just a blue screen  and i i pulled out uh on the phone  it was clear then as the screen got  wider that they were in the middle of  the aegean  my wife told me the only light  on these greeks island that we saw  now far away from us  so i told my wife tell this  driver to find the right way  otherwise you will you will not  reach this island  [Music]  i told him how bad the situation is  we see nothing just hear people crying  and  praying to god to help us i told him  about the water  that started to enter into the boat  it was so icy  another set of numbers came in and basil  said now they are here  they ask me to bring help  [Music]  and i replied that i had i had asked for  help  and the reason i was able to do that is  because i had contacts  in the area an organization called moas  who are effectively a um a private coast  guard  on the greek islands and say look i know  there's a boat out there  these are the coordinates what can you  do  so mark told me they have a ship  near of samus and it's not far away from  the location of your family  i was very worried because they are  in the middle of the sea maybe  they will lost the connection or  the mobile phone will get some more  water and  they will be damaged  her mobile phone was the only estimate  that they can use to tell us  about their location to tell us about  the whole situation  and just we had blessing in discuss  because the internet connection was so  good to still communicate  with my husband and send him our  locations  and then basil was sending me more and  more coordinates  and then he says the water level is  raised and they're afraid from the fuel  running out  the driver told us to throw every  luggage we brought with us  in the sea he tried to  decrease the load it was kind of a race  against time  me sending the coordinates to moas moa  saying yep we've got them we're trying  to get there  and then basil sending me more and more  messages which which as i say got more  and more distraught  [Music]  my wife was next to me and wondering why  i was furiously texting  and actually helping to coordinate a  rescue that was taking place  you know a thousand or so miles to the  south  remember it's pitch black they are  looking for a tiny dot on a dark horizon  every minute was for us like a day  in this case you just think whether the  boat  will still be able to bring us  to our destination we'd pass the  coordinates to moas  and within the space of i guess it was  probably 45 minutes or so  [Music]  i was getting messages back saying  they've seen a boat  when we saw the lights of the ship all  of us  turned on the church of our mobile  phones to trying  to make a visible signal that let the  people  on the ship see us quickly  we are there we are here to save you say  if you will we need to stay calm  and stay seated everybody sit down by  this stage the messages  the pings are near constant back and  forth  it's still late at night overcrowded  boat panicking people  the chances of that boat overturning  were quite high and that can happen even  when the rescue boat is closed  [Music]  at this time i was worried because my  mother and my father was so sick  so i tried to call her to send her a  message but  they my messages  weren't arrived  [Music]  i got an email from jason florio the  photographer on board the  rescue boat and it just said hi mark  all safe aboard mohawk's ship the  responder  cheers jason i screen grabbed that  email and sent it straight to basil  eventually uh within within  i guess about another half an hour we  managed to get the uh the lines of  communication  turn them into phone calls and he spoke  to his family on board the rescue boat  [Music]  uh one of the rescuers offered us his  mobile phone i  called my husband basil i said to him we  are alive  we are safe i will never ever do  something like that again  when i hear her voice it's really  was like a miracle  it was a few weeks later in early  february 2016  they were finally reunited in germany  we were on the station platform in  sarbrooken  on the platform basil waits for a train  that's late  but eventually pulls in and on the train  was his mother his father beyonce zane  and his twin sister  it was a wonderful moment actually  seeing this this family reunited after  months  there were moments clearly when basil  thought his family wouldn't make it  this isn't a one-off across europe  dislocated families are being reunited  the elderly it was in the middle of  night  and he left everything and he just sat  on his computer  get these messages for me and sent  them to to his contacts  i'd covered the refugee crisis for many  many months  and for me it was really important to  to individualize these journeys  it puts it all into a better context  zane and basil are now um living happily  in germany they were engaged  when all this took place now they're  married they've got two children  [Music]  the crossing of the mediterranean made  by zayn  is a journey migrants continue to  undertake in 2021.  migrant rescue was written and produced  by robert mulhern  for more information on this podcast go  to skynews.com  forward slash storycast 21.  next time  [Music]  we're in chile where a team of miners  find themselves trapped  more than half a mile underground in the  desert  we were just doing some job and suddenly  just a huge huge amounts of rocks like a  really huge amount of rock  coming down and we tried to run we said  that that's about it we're dead

